# Can markets and states mix well?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "How do the Nordic countries combine open markets and a big state? What does the mix tell us about the market-or-state question?"
next: {"title":"Can a market work without money?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/61-can-a-market-work-without-money.cast"}

## One line or two?
sources:
    - id: oecdrev
      title: 'Revenue Statistics: OECD member countries, comparative tax revenues (total tax revenue, % of GDP, 2023)'
      authors: OECD
      year: 2025
      finding: 'Tax revenue 2023: United States 25.6%, OECD average 33.7%, Sweden 41.7%, Denmark 44.0% of GDP.'
      url: https://sdmx.oecd.org/public/rest/data/OECD.CTP.TPS,DSD_REV_COMP_OECD@DF_RSOECD,/
    - id: oecdpmr
      title: Product Market Regulation indicators, 2023 (economy-wide PMR, 0-6)
      authors: OECD
      year: 2024
      finding: Of 56 countries scored in 2023, Sweden has the least restrictive product-market regulation (0.81); Finland 7th, Norway 8th, Denmark 9th, United States 34th (1.58).
      url: https://sdmx.oecd.org/public/rest/data/OECD.ECO.GCRD,DSD_PMR@DF_PMR,/

