    card title "One line or two?"

Can market (@draw tax tax_cap@)s and states mix well? Most arguments put them on one line: more state means less market.
    draw end_m end_s

B: So place countries on it by how much tax the state (@draw tax_marker_1@) collects. The United States: about twenty-six percent of GDP.

(@draw tax_marker_2@) The OECD average: thirty-fou (@draw tax_marker_3@)r. Sweden: forty-two. Denmark: forty-four.

B: So Denmark sits near the state end. Its firms must be smothered by rules, surely?
    highlight tax_marker_3 end_s effect circle

    pause 0.3

That's the natural guess. But a tax take measures how big the state is, not how freely markets work.
    highlight tax_cap

B: So measure markets directly. The OECD scores fifty-six countries on how much their rules hold firms back: barriers to entry, price controls, state-run companies, barriers to trade.
    draw rules rules_cap

    ask "Click on the line where you think Sweden ranks." on rules store g default "28" gap false
        intro: Rank one is the lightest touch. Where would you put Sweden?
        right: First of 56: the lightest rules of all.
        wrong: You said {g}. Big taxes, light rules: here, first of 56.

    draw rules_marker_3

B: (@draw rules_marker_1@) Finland seventh, Norway eighth, Denmark ninth. And (@draw rules_marker_2@) the United States? Thirty-fourth.

So the countries with the biggest states run some of the freest marke (@draw x_m x_s parallel true ends@)ts. One line can't hold that.

