    card title "Two dials"

One line couldn't hold the Nordics. So give each idea its own dial: the size of the state across, the weight of rules on firms going up.
    draw axes xt20 xt30 xt40 yt10 yt15 yt20

B: Thirty-eig (@draw points@)ht OECD countries, each a dot, in 2023.

If more state always meant  (@draw guide guide_lab@)more rules, the dots would climb from bottom left to top right.

B: They don't. If anything, the big-tax countries regulate firms a little less.
    highlight points

    erase guide guide_lab

Down in the bottom (@draw point_1 point_2 nordic nordic_lab@)-right corner: Denmark, Sweden, Finland, Norway. Big state, light rules.

B: And the United States sits at the (@draw point_3@) top left: a small state, but heavier rules on firms than any Nordic country.

A third sign: open (@draw trade@)ness. Denmark's exports and imports come to a hundred and thirty percent of its GDP; America's to twenty-five. Small countries always trade more, but open is open.

    erase trade

B: So who does what? Markets decide  (@draw mk@)what gets made and which firms survive. The state taxes, and insures people.

People, not firms. In 2009, when Saab was fai (@draw saab@)ling, Sweden's government refused to buy it: taxpayers, said the minister, should not own car factories.

B: But the people who lose their  (@draw q@)jobs get income and retraining. A Swedish minister put it this way: we won't protect jobs, but we will protect workers.

    erase q saab

Two dials means you can turn one without the other. Give Denmark America's rules and keep its taxes: its dot rises straight up, and doesn't move sideways.
    highlight point_1 effect circle

    animate stage 1 duration 2.5

    pause 0.5

    animate stage 0 duration 1.5

    draw point_4

    ask "Tap the country with Denmark's taxes but heavier rules." choose ["point_2","point_3","point_4"] answer point_4 voice b
        intro: Your turn: find the country with a state as big as Denmark's, but heavier rules on firms.
        right: France: taxes as high as Denmark's, and heavier rules on firms than any Nordic country.
        wrong: Look along the right-hand side, above the Nordics: here, France.

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want the number behind 'a little less'?

@math

    erase mathq

    fade axes points point_1 point_2 point_3 point_4 nordic nordic_lab mk xt20 xt30 xt40 yt10 yt15 yt20 to 0.12

A correlation meas (@draw math_box math_line_1@)ures how tightly two things move together, from minus one to plus one.

Across all thirty-eight, tax (@draw math_line_2@) take and rules on firms correlate at minus zero point four three.

Keep only the nineteen richest,  (@draw math_line_3@)above fifty thousand dollars a head: minus zero point one. Close to nothing.

So the two dials barely m (@draw math_line_4@)ove together: knowing one tells you little about the other.

    erase math

@test

    erase mathq

    fade axes points point_1 point_2 point_3 point_4 nordic nordic_lab mk xt20 xt30 xt40 yt10 yt15 yt20 to 1

