    card title "A mix that had to be tuned"

So a big state and free markets can live side by  (@draw axes line_2@)side. But has the mix always worked? Follow Sweden's tax take since 1965.

B: Thirty-one percent of GDP in 19 (@draw line_1@)65. Almost forty-nine by 1990.
    highlight line_2

Meanwhile Sweden slid. Fourth (@draw rank1@) richest of twenty-three rich countrie (@draw rank2@)s in 1970; sixteenth by 1993.

B: The economist Assar Lindbeck calle (@draw lind@)d it the Swedish experiment: high taxes, heavy rules, and subsidies for ailing firms. A banking crisis in the early nineties ended it.

    erase rank1 rank2 lind

Then Swede (@draw reform@)n re-tuned: vouchers for private schools, competition in telecoms and electricity, a new pension system, a cap on spending. Taxes peaked at fifty percent in 2000, and have drifted down to forty-two.
    animate stage 1 duration 3.5

B: So the mix that works today is a tuned one, with more room for markets than in 1990.
    highlight line_2

    erase reform

Two more doubts. Nima Sa (@draw san@)nandaji argues the Nordics were trusting, hard-working and fairly equal before the big state, so the state may get credit it hasn't earned.

B: And Acemoglu, Robinson and Verdier  (@draw arv@)ask whether cuddly Nordic capitalism lives off cutthroat America's risky inventions. Critics answer that the Nordics invent plenty themselves.

    erase san arv

How sure are we? That a big state can (@draw st1@) sit beside open, lightly regulated markets is mostly agreed: the Nordics show it can.

B: Whether the mix causes their succes (@draw st2@)s, and whether it would travel to a bigger, less trusting country, is contested.

    erase st1 st2

Its strength: mark (@draw pc@)ets pick the winners while the state shares the risk. Its weakness: it leans on trust, high employment and a willingness to pay, and no law can order those up.

    erase pc

    quiz "A country copies Nordic-sized public services but keeps price controls and state-run firms everywhere. Where does it sit on the two dials?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: Big state, heavy rules: the taxes set one dial, the price controls and state firms the other.
        wrong: Taxes set one dial; rules on firms set the other: here, big state, heavy rules.
        + Big state, heavy rules
        * Big state, light rules
        * Small state, light rules

    quiz "A country has light rules on firms, a tax take of 25% of GDP and no unemployment insurance. A big employer fails. Who carries most of the loss?" voice b
        right: The laid-off workers: light rules let the firm fail, and a small state leaves the risk with them.
        wrong: Ask who insures people when the state doesn't: here, the laid-off workers.
        + The laid-off workers
        * The state
        * The firm's rivals

    quiz "A country raises its tax take from 30% to 45% of GDP and, at the same time, scraps most price controls and state monopolies. Where does its dot move on the two dials?"
        right: Right and down: a bigger state on one dial, lighter rules on the other.
        wrong: Taxes move the dot sideways, rules on firms move it up or down: here, right and down.
        + Right and down
        * Right and up
        * Straight up

So can markets and states mix wel (@draw fin@)l? Yes, when each does what it's good at. Market or state was never one dial.

    pause 0.8

